# Every env file, whatever it is called. The named rules above cover the
# conventional ones; this catches `.env.production`, `.env.staging` and
# anything else someone invents, because the cost of missing one is a
# committed secret.
#
# `.env.example` is the deliberate exception: it carries variable names and no
# values, and it is what tells a new checkout which variables to set. It is
# already tracked, so the rule above does not hide it today -- gitignore does
# not apply to tracked files -- but it would be skipped the moment anyone
# removed and re-added it, which is exactly when nobody is looking.

.env*
*.env
!.env.example

# Per-developer agent tooling. These appear in the checkout because various
# assistants install skills, caches and symlink farms into the repo root, not
# because the project uses them. None of it is project configuration and none
# of it should reach a shared branch: it is machine-local, it changes without
# anyone editing it, and it is one `git add -A` away from being committed to a
# private repo.
#
# `.claude/`, `.factory/` and `skills/` are symlink trees into `.agents/`, so
# they are listed individually rather than covered by a glob -- a pattern loose
# enough to catch them all would also catch legitimate dot-directories.
#
# Every rule is anchored with a leading slash because these are repo-root
# artifacts. Unanchored, `skills/` matches at any depth and swallows the
# tracked `app/api/skills/` routes. Gitignore does not apply to tracked files,
# so that would look fine until someone removed and re-added a route -- which
# is exactly when nobody is looking.
/.agents/
/.claude/
/.factory/
/.hermes/
/.inferencesh/
/.pi/
/.tabnine/
/skills/
/skills-lock.json
